<pre class="r"><code>decades &lt;- c(&quot;1980s&quot;, &quot;1990s&quot;, &quot;2000s&quot;, &quot;2010s&quot;, &quot;2020s&quot;)
data_ml &lt;- data_model %&gt;% filter(decade %in% decades)

# Demographics dataset
demographic_vars &lt;- c(&quot;age&quot;, &quot;gender&quot;, &quot;race&quot;, &quot;education&quot;, &quot;region&quot;,
                       &quot;income&quot;, &quot;religion&quot;, &quot;church&quot;, &quot;marital&quot;,
                       &quot;work_status&quot;, &quot;religion_important&quot;)

data_demo &lt;- data_ml %&gt;%
   dplyr::select(party, decade, all_of(demographic_vars)) %&gt;%
  mutate(
    marital = factor(marital, levels = c(1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7),
                     labels = c(&quot;Married&quot;, &quot;Never married&quot;, &quot;Divorced&quot;,
                                &quot;Separated&quot;, &quot;Widowed&quot;, &quot;Partners&quot;)),
    work_status = factor(work_status, levels = c(1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7),
                         labels = c(&quot;Working&quot;, &quot;Temp laid off&quot;, &quot;Unemployed&quot;,
                                    &quot;Retired&quot;, &quot;Disabled&quot;, &quot;Homemaker&quot;, &quot;Student&quot;)),
    religion_important = factor(religion_important, levels = c(1, 2),
                                labels = c(&quot;Important&quot;, &quot;Not important&quot;))
  ) %&gt;%
  drop_na()

# Attitudes variables
attitude_vars_clean &lt;- c(&quot;gov_jobs&quot;, &quot;aid_black&quot;, &quot;abortion&quot;, &quot;gov_spending&quot;,
                          &quot;religion_important&quot;, &quot;moral_adjust&quot;, &quot;traditional_values&quot;,
                          &quot;equal_opportunity&quot;, &quot;not_problem_unequal&quot;,
                          &quot;worry_less_equality&quot;, &quot;fewer_problems_equal&quot;)</code></pre>
<div id="demographics-track" class="section level2">
<h2>Demographics Track</h2>
<p>We begin with the demographics track, which uses only
socio-demographic characteristics — age, gender, race, education,
religion, income, region, and church attendance, to predict party
identification. This track tests H2: <strong>whether demographic
characteristics have become more predictive of party identification over
time</strong>. The results provide a useful baseline against which to
compare the attitude-based models in the following section.</p>
<div id="logistic-regression" class="section level3">
<h3>Logistic Regression</h3>
<p>The logistic regression model demonstrates a steady improvement in
predicting accuracy over the decades, increasing from 63.6% in the 1980s
to 71.4% in the 2020s. AUC scores also show an upward trend, rising from
71.3 to 77.6 during the same time. This suggests that demographic
factors are becoming more linked to party identification, which aligns
with H2. However, the overall accuracy is still limited. Even in the
2020s, about one in three people are misclassified based on demographics
alone. Knowing someone’s age, race, income, or education doesn’t provide
a clear picture of their political party affiliation, supporting the
findings of <span class="citation">Kim and Zilinsky (2022)</span>, who
indicated that demographics reveal surprisingly little about how people
vote.</p>
<pre class="r"><code># LOGISTIC REGRESSION PER DECADE (demographics)

library(pROC)</code></pre>
<pre><code>## Type &#39;citation(&quot;pROC&quot;)&#39; for a citation.</code></pre>
<pre><code>## 
## Attaching package: &#39;pROC&#39;</code></pre>
<pre><code>## The following objects are masked from &#39;package:stats&#39;:
## 
##     cov, smooth, var</code></pre>
<pre class="r"><code>set.seed(123)
logreg_results_demo &lt;- data.frame()
for (dec in decades) {
  
  subset &lt;- data_demo %&gt;% filter(decade == dec)
  
  train_idx &lt;- createDataPartition(subset$party, p = 0.7, list = FALSE)
  train &lt;- subset[train_idx, ]
  test  &lt;- subset[-train_idx, ]
  
  log_model &lt;- glm(party ~ age + gender + race + education + region + income + 
                      religion + church + marital + work_status + religion_important,
                    data = train, family = binomial)
  
  pred_prob &lt;- predict(log_model, newdata = test, type = &quot;response&quot;)
  pred_class &lt;- factor(ifelse(pred_prob &gt; 0.5, &quot;Republican&quot;, &quot;Democrat&quot;),
                       levels = levels(test$party))
  
  cm &lt;- confusionMatrix(pred_class, test$party, positive = &quot;Republican&quot;)
  auc_val &lt;- auc(roc(as.numeric(test$party == &quot;Republican&quot;), pred_prob, quiet = TRUE))
  
  logreg_results_demo &lt;- rbind(logreg_results_demo, data.frame(
    decade = dec,
    accuracy = round(cm$overall[&quot;Accuracy&quot;] * 100, 1),
    f1 = round(cm$byClass[&quot;F1&quot;] * 100, 1),
    auc = round(auc_val * 100, 1),
    n_train = nrow(train), n_test = nrow(test)
  ))
}
rownames(logreg_results_demo) &lt;- NULL
knitr::kable(logreg_results_demo, caption = &quot;Logistic Regression Performance by Decade (Demographics Track)&quot;)</code></pre>
