</div>
<div id="decision-tree" class="section level3">
<h3>Decision Tree</h3>
<pre class="r"><code># DECISION TREE
library(rpart)
library(rpart.plot)

set.seed(123)

tree_full &lt;- rpart(
  party ~ age + gender + race + education + region + income + 
    religion + church + marital + work_status + religion_important,
  data = data_demo,
  method = &quot;class&quot;,
  control = rpart.control(
    maxdepth = 5,
    minsplit = 200,
    minbucket = 80,
    cp = 0.003
  )
)

rpart.plot(tree_full,
           type = 4,
           extra = 104,
           fallen.leaves = TRUE,
           main = &quot;Party Identification Decision Tree\n(Demographics, 1980s-2020s)&quot;,
           cex = 0.55,
           box.palette = c(&quot;#2171b5&quot;, &quot;#cb181d&quot;))</code></pre>
<p><img role="img" src="data:image/png;base64," width="672" /></p>
<p>The decision tree shows how different demographic groups tend to
vote. It highlights that Black and Hispanic respondents are mostly
classified as Democrats, while white respondents are further divided
based on factors like religious beliefs, church attendance, gender, and
education. This pattern reflects the way race influences American
politics and the growing role of religious identity.</p>
<p>However, decision trees have a drawback: they can be unstable. Small
changes in the data or how it’s split into training and testing sets can
lead to different results throughout the tree. This is why we will look
at Random Forest next, which helps address this issue.</p>
<pre class="r"><code># DECISION TREE PER DECADE (demographics)
set.seed(123)
dt_results_demo &lt;- data.frame()

for (dec in decades) {
  
  subset &lt;- data_demo %&gt;% filter(decade == dec)
  
  train_idx &lt;- createDataPartition(subset$party, p = 0.7, list = FALSE)
  train &lt;- subset[train_idx, ]
  test  &lt;- subset[-train_idx, ]
  
  tree_model &lt;- rpart(
    party ~ age + gender + race + education + region + income + 
      religion + church + marital + work_status + religion_important,
    data = train, method = &quot;class&quot;,
    control = rpart.control(maxdepth = 5, minsplit = 100, minbucket = 50, cp = 0.005)
  )
  
  pred &lt;- predict(tree_model, newdata = test, type = &quot;class&quot;)
  acc &lt;- mean(pred == test$party)
  
  dt_results_demo &lt;- rbind(dt_results_demo, data.frame(
    decade = dec, model = &quot;Decision Tree&quot;,
    accuracy = round(acc * 100, 1),
    n_train = nrow(train), n_test = nrow(test)
  ))
  
  cat(paste0(dec, &quot; — DT: &quot;, round(acc * 100, 1), &quot;%\n&quot;))
}</code></pre>
<pre><code>## 1980s — DT: 64.1%
## 1990s — DT: 64.8%
## 2000s — DT: 67.1%
## 2010s — DT: 70.8%
## 2020s — DT: 69%</code></pre>
<pre class="r"><code>print(dt_results_demo)</code></pre>
