<pre class="r"><code># Check number of trees — demographics RF
plot(rf_model, main = &quot;RF Error by Number of Trees (Demographics)&quot;)</code></pre>
<p><img role="img" src="data:image/png;base64," width="672" /></p>
<p>The plot above shows the out-of-bag error rate as a function of the
number of trees, using the 2020s decade as a representative example. The
error curve flattens well before 500 trees, confirming that ntree = 500
is a sufficient and stable choice. Increasing the number of trees
further would not meaningfully improve model performance.</p>
<pre class="r"><code>#  ROC CURVES + CONFUSION MATRIX: 1980s 
library(pROC)

# Attitudes models (1980s)
subset_raw &lt;- data_ml %&gt;%
  filter(decade == &quot;1980s&quot;) %&gt;%
   dplyr::select(party, all_of(attitude_vars_clean)) %&gt;%
  filter(!is.na(party))

data_imp &lt;- complete(mice(subset_raw, m = 1, maxit = 10,
                           method = &quot;pmm&quot;, seed = 123, printFlag = FALSE), 1)

set.seed(123)
idx &lt;- createDataPartition(data_imp$party, p = 0.7, list = FALSE)
train_att &lt;- data_imp[idx, ]
test_att  &lt;- data_imp[-idx, ]

# Demographics models (1980s)
subset_demo &lt;- data_demo %&gt;% filter(decade == &quot;1980s&quot;)

set.seed(123)
idx2 &lt;- createDataPartition(subset_demo$party, p = 0.7, list = FALSE)
train_demo &lt;- subset_demo[idx2, ]
test_demo  &lt;- subset_demo[-idx2, ]

# LogReg demographics
log_demo &lt;- glm(party ~ age + gender + race + education + region + income +
                  religion + church + marital + work_status + religion_important,
                data = train_demo, family = binomial)
log_demo_prob &lt;- predict(log_demo, test_demo, type = &quot;response&quot;)
log_demo_pred &lt;- factor(ifelse(log_demo_prob &gt; 0.5, &quot;Republican&quot;, &quot;Democrat&quot;),
                        levels = levels(test_demo$party))
cat(&quot;\n--- Demographics: Logistic Regression ---\n&quot;)</code></pre>
