<div id="demographics-summary-plot" class="section level3">
<h3>Demographics Summary Plot</h3>
<pre class="r"><code># Combined demographics plot: all 3 models 
all_demo &lt;- rbind(
  dt_results_demo %&gt;% dplyr::select(decade, model = model, accuracy),
  logreg_results_demo %&gt;% mutate(model = &quot;Logistic Regression&quot;) %&gt;% select(decade, model, accuracy),
  rf_results_demo %&gt;% mutate(model = &quot;Random Forest&quot;) %&gt;% select(decade, model, accuracy)
)
all_demo$decade &lt;- factor(all_demo$decade, levels = decades)
all_demo$model &lt;- factor(all_demo$model, 
                          levels = c(&quot;Logistic Regression&quot;, &quot;Decision Tree&quot;, &quot;Random Forest&quot;))

demo_colors &lt;- c(&quot;Logistic Regression&quot; = &quot;#2d8659&quot;, 
                  &quot;Decision Tree&quot; = &quot;#e6994a&quot;,
                  &quot;Random Forest&quot; = &quot;#7b68ee&quot;)

ggplot(all_demo, aes(x = decade, y = accuracy, color = model, group = model)) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.2) +
  geom_point(size = 3) +
  geom_text(aes(label = paste0(accuracy, &quot;%&quot;)), vjust = -1, size = 3) +
  scale_color_manual(values = demo_colors) +
  scale_y_continuous(limits = c(55, 80), labels = function(x) paste0(x, &quot;%&quot;)) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 50, linetype = &quot;dashed&quot;, color = &quot;gray50&quot;, alpha = 0.5) +
  labs(title = &quot;Demographics-Only Models: Accuracy by Decade&quot;,
       subtitle = &quot;All models show increasing accuracy → demographics increasingly predict party ID (H2)&quot;,
       x = &quot;&quot;, y = &quot;Test Accuracy&quot;, color = &quot;Model&quot;) +
  theme_party</code></pre>
<pre><code>## Warning: Removed 1 row containing missing values or values outside the scale range
## (`geom_hline()`).</code></pre>
<p><img role="img" src="data:image/png;base64," width="672" /></p>
<p>Demographics-only accuracy increases from approximately 64% in the
1980s to 71% in the 2020s across all three model types. This ~7
percentage point gain over four decades suggests that demographic
characteristics have become modestly more predictive of party
identification, consistent with H2. However, the rate of increase is
smaller than for the attitudes track (below), suggesting that
attitudinal sorting, rather than demographic realignment, is the primary
driver of the overall polarization trend.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="attitudes-track" class="section level2">
<h2>Attitudes Track</h2>
<p>We now focus on how people’s policy attitudes influence their party
identification instead of looking at demographics. This is the main part
of our analysis. If ideological sorting has grown over time, then
understanding someone’s beliefs about issues like abortion, government
spending, or racial equality should better predict their political party
in 2024 compared to the 1980s.</p>
<pre class="r"><code>library(mice)
library(dplyr)
library(tidyr)

attitude_vars_clean &lt;- c(&quot;gov_jobs&quot;, &quot;aid_black&quot;, &quot;abortion&quot;, &quot;gov_spending&quot;,
                          &quot;religion_important&quot;, &quot;moral_adjust&quot;, &quot;traditional_values&quot;,
                          &quot;equal_opportunity&quot;, &quot;not_problem_unequal&quot;,
                          &quot;worry_less_equality&quot;, &quot;fewer_problems_equal&quot;)

data_ml %&gt;%
  filter(decade %in% decades) %&gt;%
  group_by(decade) %&gt;%
  summarise(across(all_of(attitude_vars_clean), 
                   ~round(mean(!is.na(.)) * 100, 0))) %&gt;%
  pivot_longer(-decade, names_to = &quot;variable&quot;, values_to = &quot;pct_available&quot;) %&gt;%
  pivot_wider(names_from = decade, values_from = pct_available) %&gt;%
  print(n = 15)</code></pre>
<pre><code>## # A tibble: 11 × 6
##    variable             `1980s` `1990s` `2000s` `2010s` `2020s`
##    &lt;chr&gt;                  &lt;dbl&gt;   &lt;dbl&gt;   &lt;dbl&gt;   &lt;dbl&gt;   &lt;dbl&gt;
##  1 gov_jobs                  75      90      44      92      91
##  2 aid_black                 86      92      57      89      90
##  3 abortion                  97      98      56      99      94
##  4 gov_spending              70      87      42      89      87
##  5 religion_important        65      97     100     100     100
##  6 moral_adjust              41      84      68      90      88
##  7 traditional_values        42      84      68      90      88
##  8 equal_opportunity         65      71      68      90      90
##  9 not_problem_unequal       65      71      68      90      90
## 10 worry_less_equality       65      71      68      90      90
## 11 fewer_problems_equal      65      85      68      90      90</code></pre>
<div id="logistic-regression-with-mice-imputation" class="section level3">
<h3>Logistic Regression with MICE Imputation</h3>
<p>The results are significant. Accuracy has increased from 67.8% in the
1980s to 85.9% in the 2020s—an 18 percentage point increase over four
decades. The AUC scores reflect a similar trend, rising from 73.7 to
93.0. In comparison, demographic tracking has improved by only about 7
percentage points during the same period. The attitude-based models are
not only better but are also improving at more than twice the rate. By
the 2020s, the F1 score reaches 85.1, indicating that the model reliably
classifies both Democrats and Republicans with high and nearly equal
accuracy. This provides strong support for the hypothesis that
ideological sorting has increased significantly, driven by attitudes
rather than demographics.</p>
<pre class="r"><code>library(caret)
library(pROC)
set.seed(123)
logreg_results_att &lt;- data.frame()
for (dec in decades) {
  
  subset_raw &lt;- data_ml %&gt;%
    filter(decade == dec) %&gt;%
    select(party, all_of(attitude_vars_clean)) %&gt;%
    filter(!is.na(party))
  
  imp &lt;- mice(subset_raw, m = 5, maxit = 10, method = &quot;pmm&quot;, 
              seed = 123, printFlag = FALSE)
  
  acc_all &lt;- c()
  f1_all &lt;- c()
  auc_all &lt;- c()
  
  for (i in 1:5) {
    data_imp &lt;- complete(imp, i)
    
    train_idx &lt;- createDataPartition(data_imp$party, p = 0.7, list = FALSE)
    train &lt;- data_imp[train_idx, ]
    test  &lt;- data_imp[-train_idx, ]
    
    log_model &lt;- glm(party ~ ., data = train, family = binomial)
    pred_prob &lt;- predict(log_model, newdata = test, type = &quot;response&quot;)
    pred_class &lt;- factor(ifelse(pred_prob &gt; 0.5, &quot;Republican&quot;, &quot;Democrat&quot;),
                         levels = levels(test$party))
    
    cm &lt;- confusionMatrix(pred_class, test$party, positive = &quot;Republican&quot;)
    auc_val &lt;- auc(roc(as.numeric(test$party == &quot;Republican&quot;), pred_prob, quiet = TRUE))
    
    acc_all &lt;- c(acc_all, cm$overall[&quot;Accuracy&quot;])
    f1_all &lt;- c(f1_all, cm$byClass[&quot;F1&quot;])
    auc_all &lt;- c(auc_all, as.numeric(auc_val))
  }
  
  logreg_results_att &lt;- rbind(logreg_results_att, data.frame(
    decade = dec, model = &quot;Logistic Regression&quot;,
    accuracy = round(mean(acc_all) * 100, 1),
    f1 = round(mean(f1_all) * 100, 1),
    auc = round(mean(auc_all) * 100, 1),
    n_total = nrow(subset_raw)
  ))
}
rownames(logreg_results_att) &lt;- NULL
knitr::kable(logreg_results_att)</code></pre>
