</div>
<div id="logistic-regression-coefficient-heatmap" class="section level3">
<h3>Logistic Regression Coefficient Heatmap</h3>
<pre class="r"><code>coef_att_list &lt;- list()

for (dec in decades) {
  
  subset_raw &lt;- data_ml %&gt;%
    filter(decade == dec) %&gt;%
    select(party, all_of(attitude_vars_clean)) %&gt;%
    filter(!is.na(party))
  
  imp &lt;- mice(subset_raw, m = 5, maxit = 10, method = &quot;pmm&quot;,
              seed = 123, printFlag = FALSE)
  
  coef_all &lt;- list()
  for (i in 1:5) {
    data_imp &lt;- complete(imp, i)
    log_model &lt;- glm(party ~ ., data = data_imp, family = binomial)
    coef_all[[i]] &lt;- coef(log_model)[-1]
  }
  
  avg_coef &lt;- Reduce(&quot;+&quot;, coef_all) / 5
  
  coef_att_list[[dec]] &lt;- data.frame(
    decade = dec,
    variable = names(avg_coef),
    coefficient = as.numeric(avg_coef)
  )
}

coef_att_df &lt;- bind_rows(coef_att_list)
coef_att_df$decade &lt;- factor(coef_att_df$decade, levels = decades)

var_labels_att &lt;- c(
  &quot;gov_jobs&quot; = &quot;Gov&#39;t Jobs&quot;, &quot;aid_black&quot; = &quot;Aid to Black Americans&quot;,
  &quot;abortion&quot; = &quot;Abortion&quot;, &quot;gov_spending&quot; = &quot;Gov&#39;t Spending&quot;,
  &quot;religion_important&quot; = &quot;Religion Important&quot;, &quot;moral_adjust&quot; = &quot;Moral Standards&quot;,
  &quot;traditional_values&quot; = &quot;Traditional Values&quot;, &quot;equal_opportunity&quot; = &quot;Equal Opportunity&quot;,
  &quot;not_problem_unequal&quot; = &quot;Not Problem if Unequal&quot;,
  &quot;worry_less_equality&quot; = &quot;Worry Less Equality&quot;,
  &quot;fewer_problems_equal&quot; = &quot;Fewer Problems if Equal&quot;
)
coef_att_df$label &lt;- var_labels_att[coef_att_df$variable]

var_order &lt;- coef_att_df %&gt;%
  group_by(label) %&gt;%
  summarise(avg = mean(abs(coefficient))) %&gt;%
  arrange(avg) %&gt;%
  pull(label)
coef_att_df$label &lt;- factor(coef_att_df$label, levels = var_order)

ggplot(coef_att_df, aes(x = decade, y = label, fill = coefficient)) +
  geom_tile(color = &quot;white&quot;, linewidth = 0.5) +
  scale_fill_gradient2(low = &quot;#2171b5&quot;, mid = &quot;white&quot;, high = &quot;#cb181d&quot;,
                       midpoint = 0, name = &quot;Coefficient\n(+ = Republican)&quot;) +
  geom_text(aes(label = round(coefficient, 2)), size = 2.8) +
  labs(title = &quot;How Attitudes Predict Party ID Across Decades&quot;,
       subtitle = &quot;Logistic regression coefficients | Blue = predicts Democrat, Red = predicts Republican&quot;,
       x = &quot;&quot;, y = &quot;&quot;) +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme(plot.title = element_text(face = &quot;bold&quot;, size = 14),
        plot.subtitle = element_text(color = &quot;gray40&quot;, size = 10),
        axis.text.y = element_text(size = 10))</code></pre>
<p><img role="img" src="data:image/png;base64," width="672" /></p>
<p>To understand what factors are leading to improved Democratic
identification, we look at how individual attitudes have changed over
the decades. The heatmap shows some key trends.
<strong>Abortion</strong> has seen the biggest shift, with its impact
growing from almost zero in the 1980s to a strong negative coefficient
of -0.65 in the 2020s. This makes it the strongest predictor of
Democratic identification by the end of this period (a negative value
means more pro-choice views are linked to more Democratic
identification). <strong>Government spending preferences</strong> have
also become more supportive of Democratic views.</p>
<p>Meanwhile, attitudes around <strong>traditional values</strong> and
<strong>moral standards</strong> have consistently leaned Republican,
with their influence increasing over time. In contrast, economic factors
such as government job guarantees and equal opportunity have remained
relatively stable and modest in impact across all decades. This
partially supports the idea that cultural and moral issues have become
more important in shaping political identification than economic issues,
but this trend isn’t consistent for all factors.</p>
</div>
<div id="lasso" class="section level3">
<h3>LASSO</h3>
<p>The LASSO results support the findings from the logistic regression.
Unlike older models that would reduce weak predictors to zero, LASSO
keeps all 11 variables in most decades. This shows that each attitude
item has its own predictive value. The agreement between these two
different methods boosts our confidence that the trend we see is a real
change in the relationship between attitudes and political parties, not
just due to how the model is set up.</p>
<pre class="r"><code># LASSO: Attitudes per decade 
library(glmnet)</code></pre>
<pre><code>## Loading required package: Matrix</code></pre>
<pre><code>## 
## Attaching package: &#39;Matrix&#39;</code></pre>
<pre><code>## The following objects are masked from &#39;package:tidyr&#39;:
## 
##     expand, pack, unpack</code></pre>
<pre><code>## Loaded glmnet 4.1-10</code></pre>
<pre class="r"><code>set.seed(123)
lasso_coefs &lt;- list()
lasso_results &lt;- data.frame()
for (dec in decades) {
  
  subset_raw &lt;- data_ml %&gt;%
    filter(decade == dec) %&gt;%
    select(party, all_of(attitude_vars_clean)) %&gt;%
    filter(!is.na(party))
  
  data_imp &lt;- complete(mice(subset_raw, m = 1, maxit = 10,
                             method = &quot;pmm&quot;, seed = 123, printFlag = FALSE), 1)
  
  idx &lt;- createDataPartition(data_imp$party, p = 0.7, list = FALSE)
  train &lt;- data_imp[idx, ]
  test  &lt;- data_imp[-idx, ]
  
  x_train &lt;- as.matrix(train[, -1])
  y_train &lt;- as.numeric(train$party == &quot;Republican&quot;)
  x_test  &lt;- as.matrix(test[, -1])
  y_test  &lt;- as.numeric(test$party == &quot;Republican&quot;)
  
  cv_lasso &lt;- cv.glmnet(x_train, y_train, family = &quot;binomial&quot;, alpha = 1, nfolds = 10)
  
  pred_prob &lt;- predict(cv_lasso, x_test, s = &quot;lambda.min&quot;, type = &quot;response&quot;)
  pred_class &lt;- factor(ifelse(pred_prob &gt; 0.5, &quot;Republican&quot;, &quot;Democrat&quot;),
                       levels = levels(test$party))
  
  acc &lt;- mean(pred_class == test$party)
  
  coefs &lt;- coef(cv_lasso, s = &quot;lambda.min&quot;)
  coef_df &lt;- data.frame(
    decade = dec,
    variable = rownames(coefs)[-1],
    coefficient = as.numeric(coefs)[-1]
  )
  lasso_coefs[[dec]] &lt;- coef_df
  
  lasso_results &lt;- rbind(lasso_results, data.frame(
    decade = dec, accuracy = round(acc * 100, 1)
  ))
}
knitr::kable(lasso_results)</code></pre>
